{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 240,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Anyesk for supporting PBS. In \n1987, Steven Weinberg wrote a cute little paper"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6000,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entitled anthropic bound on the cosmological \nconstant. I say cute and little because this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13040,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "work feels minor in comparison to say electroeak \nunification theory that won him the Nobel Prize."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20480,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Weineberg was foundational in establishing the \nstandard model of particle physics and represented"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25440,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an enormous leap in our understanding of how \nthis universe works. But his little 1987 paper,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34160,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "although more obscure, may tell us something \nabout how the multiverse works and can even"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39760,
    "dDurationMs": 9000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be thought of as evidence for the existence of \nan enormous number of other universes. [Music]"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52720,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey everyone, before we get started, I wanted \nto let you know that August 8th was the 94th"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57680,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "birthday of Sir Roger Penrose. The work of Sir \nRoger and his famous Penrose diagram have been"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63600,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an important part of spaceime. And to celebrate, \nwe have a limited edition Penrose diagram pin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69440,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This pin is based off our original design for the \nPenrose diagram. The pin will only be available"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75280,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "via pre-sale through midepptember. We're also \noffering 10% off for the first 24 hours as well"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81680,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as 5% off all Penrose inspired merch. There are \nlinks to the merch store in the description. Now,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88720,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on to the episode. In our last episode, we \nasked whether it's bad science to hypothesize a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94400,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multiverse. We concluded that it very much depends \non where the particular multiverse comes from. But"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100560,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in general, we concluded that if a theory predicts \na multiverse but isn't itself concocted with the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106960,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intention of producing a multiverse, then we can't \njust necessarily dismiss the theory on aesthetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112320,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "grounds, for example, for a violation of Okam's \nrazor. But that doesn't help with the testability"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118480,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the hypothesis. If we can't ever travel \nto or receive a signal from another universe,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124560,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how do we know they exist? Falsifiability is \nanother hallmark of good scientific hypothesis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132000,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And so the multiverse is in danger on that front. \nBut actually there are ways to test the multiverse"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139680,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hypothesis without ever leaving our universe. \nOur universe has several properties that seem"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particularly well suited or finely tuned for the \nformation of life. If the binding energy of helium"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152160,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was a bit lower or a bit higher, then we'd have \neither only hydrogen or no hydrogen, respectively."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159200,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If a very specific nuclear energy level of \nthe carbon 12 nucleus was a bit different,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165440,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars wouldn't produce an abundant supply of \nthis life critical element. Another example"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171600,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of an apparent fine-tuning that we've talked about \nrecently is the small mass of the Higs Bzon, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179680,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for reasons we discussed and will review seems \noddly tintsy. Related to this is the smallest"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187600,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the strength of gravity compared to the other \nforces. If either the Higs mass or the strength of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192080,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravity were larger, matter would scrunch up into \nblobs too dense to form interesting structures or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197760,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just into black holes. The separation in scale \nbetween the Higs mass and the plank scale and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203760,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the weakness of gravity and the other forces are \nboth examples of the hierarchy problem. Another"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210720,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "example is the apparently very tiny value of the \ncosmological constant. The cosmological constant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a number that appears in the Einstein \nfield equations of general relativity which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if positive causes accelerating expansion of the \nuniverse. The expansion of the universe is indeed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228960,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accelerating. And so the cosmological constant \nis indeed positive. We call this effect dark"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234640,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy. And while we don't know what it really \nis, the most routine explanation is that empty"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240000,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space has a constant energy density. This is \nsort of maybe expected due to the Heisenberg"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247200,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uncertainty principle. Even a perfect vacuum \ncan't be considered to have exactly zero energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253120,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and positive energy density of the vacuum has the \neffect of causing accelerating expansion. We've"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259280,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now seen that our universe is accelerating which \nmeans that we know that something like this vacuum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265440,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy exists and we know how strong it is. In the \nmodern universe, there's more of this dark energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than all of the other forms of energy combined \nby a factor of nearly 2 and a half. That sounds"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278080,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like a lot, but it corresponds to a minuscule \nenergy density of empty space. The only reason"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark energy is so dominant is that after nearly \n14 billion years of expansion, there's just so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289760,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much empty space that the dark energy adds up. \nOkay, so our universe seems to have this tiny"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296960,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy density of the vacuum. If we go ahead and \nuse admittedly naive methods to calculate what"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the energy of the vacuum should be based on the \nexpected activity in the known quantum fields,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310000,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we get a number that's quite a bit larger than \nwe observe, 120 orders of magnitude larger."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While this has been called the worst prediction \nin physics, we don't really presume that we can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322240,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "predict the energy of the vacuum using current \ntheories. The quantum field theory used to do"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327840,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this calculation is incomplete. It breaks down at \nvery high energies. Now, it's generally assumed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333840,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that other effects at play at higher energies than \nwe've tested work to suppress the vacuum energy,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341440,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but we don't know how. Perhaps you recall \nthe recent episode where we talked about"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how a naive attempt to calculate the mass of \nthe Higs particle using quantum field theory"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352720,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gives a similar problem. a very large or even an \ninfinite Higs mass. It's really the same issue"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359280,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and perhaps it has the same solution. One way \nto get both the Higs mass and the cosmological"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365600,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constant down to the tiny values that we observe \nin this universe is for the various effects of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371760,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the quantum fluctuations to cancel each other \nout. We know this happens with other particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377120,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like the electron in which the symmetry connected \nto the existence of antimatter sort of eliminates"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "higher energy terms in the electrons mass. The \nhypothetical particles of super symmetry were"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389920,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meant to do the same for the Higs. But we're \nhaving a hard time finding them. Absent such"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394240,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a perfect or near-perfect cancellation due to an \nunderlying symmetry. It seems that cancellation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399520,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has to be kind of random. But to randomly cancel \na bunch of uncorrelated effects down to one part"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407360,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in 10 the^ of 120 in the case of dark energy \nreally strains belief. Getting that result by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413760,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chance has the same probability as flipping heads \non a fair coin 400 times in a row. So if there was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422080,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only ever one big bang and the cosmological \nconstant could have landed anywhere in these"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "120 orders of magnitude, it feels lucky that it \nlanded where it did. Very lucky in fact because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433280,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if the cosmological constant was much higher, \nthe universe would have inflated so quickly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that no stars or galaxies could ever have formed. \nAnd if the Higs mass had been much larger, all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443600,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter would have collapsed under its own weight. \nEither way, we wouldn't have scientists to wonder"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448400,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "why these numbers have the values that they do. We \ncan get around this using the anthropic principle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454000,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by noting that if you have enough big bangs, \neventually you'll get one with the parameters you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458640,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need to form scientists. So, at least 10 the^ of \n120 of them, if that's really the possible range"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464480,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the cosmological constant. Many scientists \nhate this use of the anthropic principle in a vast"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470080,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multiverse because it seems like an explanatory \ndead end, even if it's right. They'd rather be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476000,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "able to calculate the value of the cosmological \nconstant from first principles. But there's no"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "guarantee that this is even possible. And \nactually, it turns out that we can at least"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487360,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constrain its possible value by using anthropic \narguments. So back to Steven Weinberg's paper,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495280,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entropic bound on the cosmosual constant is aptly \nnamed in it. Weineberg uses anthropic arguments to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501600,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "calculate the value of the cosmological constant \nor at least what it should have if its value in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507280,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our universe results from a selection effect \nin a multiverse of many different cosmological"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519305,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constants and only a small fraction of those \nhave values amenable for the development of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523625,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "life. We are of course in one of those. This is \nthe standard argument of the anthropic principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528105,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Weineberg goes much further. He uses this \nargument to actually estimate what that constant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533065,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should be for this universe. And the key to doing \nthis is the use of the principle of mediocrity. It"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540905,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "says that if an object or phenomenon is randomly \nselected from a collection of various categories,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547545,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it is statistically more likely to originate \nfrom the most numerous categories rather than"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552665,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the less common ones. So we can divide \nup possible universes into categories based"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558745,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the size of their cosmological constant. \nMany multiverse generating theories predict"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563625,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a fairly even distribution for this value. \nwhat we call a flat prior. Meaning there are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569865,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more categories with cosmological constants much \nmuch larger than the tiny one in our universe. The"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575465,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mediocrity principle tells us that if we choose \na random universe out of the entire multiverse,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581065,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it should have a non-special value for that \ncosmological constant. So it should have a large"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585705,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one. But if we choose a universe that we know has \nobservers in it, we add an extra constraint. We're"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594265,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now selecting from a subset of the multiverse with \na much smaller cosmotu constant. But besides the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601625,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anthropic constraint, the cosmotu constant in this \nsubset should be as non-special as possible within"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609145,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the constrained range. So says the mediocrity \nprinciple. In other words, it should not be a lot"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616745,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smaller than it needs to be to allow observers. If \nthe cosmological constant is much smaller than is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623225,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strictly needed, that might be taken as evidence \nagainst the anthropic explanation. Okay. The next"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629865,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "step then is to calculate the maximum strength \ndark energy could have before life becomes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635625,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impossible. The principle of mediocrity says \nthat dark energy shouldn't be too much smaller"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640585,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than that. Steven Weineberg did this by figuring \nout the maximum dark energy strength that would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645465,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still allow life supporting structures to form. We \nknow that stars and galaxies formed when overdense"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651305,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regions of gas and dark matter that filled the \nearly universe collapsed under their own gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 656905,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this gravitational inpole had competition. \nThe expansion of the universe meant that over"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661465,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time matter was thrown further and further apart \nand beyond a certain distance, gravity becomes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666265,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "too weak to drag that material back together. \nIt was this balance between in pulling gravity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671225,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and out pushing expansion that determines \nthe largest sizes of structures that could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676105,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "form. In the case of our universe, that's galaxy \nclusters. In our universe, dark energy started"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682665,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to dominate relatively late, kicking off the \naccelerating expansion 6 or 7 billion years ago."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689865,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But importantly, after the universe had already \nbuilt galaxies and clusters. But if you dial up"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695785,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the strength of dark energy, then it starts to \ndominate the universe earlier. And if you dial"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701705,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it up too much, then the accelerating expansion \nstarts early enough to mess with galaxy formation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707625,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can calculate a pretty straightforward limit \nto how strong dark energy can be to even get"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712985,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proper galaxies. So let's add one more factor. \nClearly, we need stars to live, but we do also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720185,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need galaxies. In order to have planets, multiple \ngenerations of stars need to have existed prior"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724825,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to our own because those earlier stars created \nthe elements needed to build planets. So we need"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730825,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star making factories which means galaxies or at \nleast small ones. For example, a globular cluster"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736905,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with a million stars might be enough. Weineberg \ncalculated that the maximum amount of dark energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 742585,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for which such systems could form is around 500 \ntimes the energy that is in matter if extrapolated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751305,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the modern era. Now the true value is that dark \nenergy is about 2.3 times larger than the energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758665,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in matter but 2.3 is quite a bit smaller than 500. \nSo from this calculation our universe still has an"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767145,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unusually small cosmological constant compared to \nthe set of all universes that could have produced"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774585,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "life or at least galaxies. A couple of things \nhere. First is that this brings the chance of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780585,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our cosmological constant being so small down from \n1 in 10 the^ of 120 to 1 in 200 which is a huge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788665,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "improvement. By adding the anthropic selection, it \nbrings the random chance hypothesis into the realm"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795625,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of possibility. Still, if the anthropic argument \ndoesn't fully solve the problem, it's not exactly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802345,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a parimonious solution. The mismatch gets worse if \nwe update some of the observations that went into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809785,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Weineberg's calculation. He used our most distant \nobservations of massive objects to determine the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815865,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "maximum time allowed to form massive structures \nin 1987. Those were quazars that existed when the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824185,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe was around 10% of its current age. But \nwe now know that there were massive structures"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830105,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much earlier. For example, the James Webb Space \nTelescope has found galaxies at around 2% of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835625,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe's current age. Those structures formed \nin an era when dark energy was truly negligible,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842265,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "indicating that dark energy could be quite a \nbit larger than it is in this universe while"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848185,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still allowing at least some galaxies to form. \nBut there's another refinement to Weineberg's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853305,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "calculation that can make sense of this. The way \nWeineberg used the mediocrity principle was to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858985,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "say that we should be in the most typical type \nof universe that can form galaxies. But a more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864025,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "powerful approach might be to apply the mediocracy \nprinciple to the class of observer rather than the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869545,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "class of universe. This idea is sometimes called \nthe self- sampling assumption which is that all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 875545,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other things being equal, an observer should \nreason as if they are randomly selected from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880825,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the set of all actually existent observers past, \npresent and future in their reference class. For"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887385,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "example, we might expect that we humanity are \na fairly typical example of all the species"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894585,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "across the multiverse that can produce scientists \ncapable of anthropic reasoning. Some universes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900745,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may produce many more such species than others. \nAnd so we might expect to find ourselves in one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906585,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of those. We could also argue that this would be \nin a universe that has more and bigger galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913465,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And more and bigger galaxies will form when dark \nenergy is weaker. In fact, if scientists producing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920745,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "civilizations are rare enough, you might need a \nuniverse with lots of bigger galaxies to even have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926505,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a chance of producing some. All of this pushes \nthe anthropic preference to smaller values of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933705,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmological constant. To really know whether \nanthropic selection is the reason behind our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939065,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "small cosmological constant or indeed any of the \nother apparently finely tuned parameters of our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944505,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe, we need a much better understanding of \nall the contingencies behind the development of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950265,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "life. But it's still remarkable that Weineberg \nwas able to guesstimate the strength of vacuum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956345,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy to within a relatively small range only \nbased on anthropic arguments. And this is the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964265,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "real kicker. When Weineberg made this calculation, \nwe didn't know that dark energy even existed. The"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972105,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accelerating expansion was discovered in the late \n'9s, a decade after Weineberg's paper. Back then,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979065,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most people thought that the cosmological constant \nwas almost certainly zero, indicating a perfect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983865,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cancellation by unknown symmetries. The fact that \nWeineberg even came within the ballpark of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990105,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right number before that number was known is seen \nby some as a powerful argument for the anthropic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995305,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "selection. And let me be clear, the anthropic \nargument only works if there's a very large"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1001305,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multiverse, which is why some might take this as \na point in favor of the multiverse hypothesis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008745,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because if all universes are possible, then we \nneed a lot of them to explain our not too rapidly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1016025,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expanding spaceime. Thank you to Anyesk for \nsupporting PBS. Anyes was created because whether"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1023945,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you're in remote locations or just working from \nhome, the need for reliable, secure communication"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1029945,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and data transfer remains important. With any \ndesk, secure direct file transfer bypasses the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036585,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cloud entirely, allowing you to move large \nmedia files, benchmarks, logs, or firmware"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1041705,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "updates between systems. And if you're creating \nremotely, any supports remote video editing, so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1048505,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can access your high performance editing rig \nremotely. They also offer audio pass through which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1055225,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means you can scrub and edit audio in real time. \nAny is available for personal use, but if your"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061625,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "research requires a lot of global coordination, \nany offers customtailored business plans. To"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1068185,
    "dDurationMs": 23739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "learn more about NADES, go to nesk.com/spacetime \nor check out the link in the description. [Music]"
    } ]
  } ]
}
